# Performance Benchmark Framework

A lightweight benchmarking framework for evaluating the performance of code across different implementations, environments, or workloads.

## Overview

This project provides a simple structure for running benchmark tests, collecting timing data, and comparing results consistently. It is designed to help measure execution time, detect regressions, and make performance trade-offs visible when optimizing code.

## Features

- Run benchmarks for multiple implementations or configurations
- Measure execution time and throughput consistently
- Support custom iterations and warm-up runs
- Aggregate and compare results across test cases
- Easy to extend with new benchmark scenarios
- Suitable for quick local performance analysis and CI-friendly checks

## Project Structure

The project is organized around a benchmark runner and reusable test cases. Common areas include:

- benchmark definitions
- execution logic for test runs
- result aggregation and reporting
- configuration for repeated iterations or sample sizes

## Getting Started

1. Clone the repository.
2. Open the project in your preferred development environment.
3. Review or add benchmark scenarios under the benchmark definitions.
4. Run the benchmark framework.
5. Inspect the output statistics and compare results across runs.

## Example Workflow

```text
configure benchmark
warm up runtime
run N iterations
collect elapsed time per run
calculate average, median, min, and max
report findings
```

## Typical Use Cases

- Comparing algorithm complexity across implementations
- Measuring API or service responsiveness
- Validating optimization changes
- Estimating runtime cost for workloads under realistic conditions

## Best Practices

- Run benchmarks multiple times to reduce noise
- Use warm-up iterations before collecting measurements
- Keep test inputs consistent between runs
- Measure the whole scenario, not just isolated operations
- Track changes over time to spot regressions or improvements

## Notes

This framework is intentionally simple and adaptable. It can be extended for language-specific runtimes, CI pipelines, or richer reporting formats such as CSV, JSON, or HTML.

